  "content": {
    "id": "p3-q14-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "14",
    "prompt": "Determine all solutions $x\\in\\mathbb R$ to\n$$(x^2-7x+11)^{x^2-11x+30}=1.$$",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$x=2,3,4,5,6$.\n\nConsider base $1$, base $-1$, and exponent $0$ with a nonzero base.",
      "worked": "If the base is $1$,\n$$\\begin{align*}x^2-7x+11&=1\\\\x^2-7x+10&=0\\\\(x-2)(x-5)&=0\\\\x&=2\\text{ or }5.\\end{align*}$$\nBoth values work because the base is $1$.\n\nIf the base is $-1$,\n$$\\begin{align*}x^2-7x+11&=-1\\\\x^2-7x+12&=0\\\\(x-3)(x-4)&=0\\\\x&=3\\text{ or }4.\\end{align*}$$\nAt $x=3$ the exponent is $9-33+30=6$; at $x=4$ it is $16-44+30=2$. Both are even integers, so both values work.\n\nFor a nonzero base other than $1$ or $-1$, the exponent must be zero:\n$$\\begin{align*}x^2-11x+30&=0\\\\(x-5)(x-6)&=0\\\\x&=5\\text{ or }6.\\end{align*}$$\nThe corresponding bases are $1$ and $5$, both nonzero. Thus $x=5$ is already included and $x=6$ also works. A zero base cannot give $1$.\n\nTherefore the complete solution set is $\\{2,3,4,5,6\\}$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 164
  },
